  <h2>How to read these so the idea lands</h2>
  <p>Three moves turn any of these from a nice story into a lasting habit:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Pause at the contrast, do not explain it.</strong> When the moon
      shines against a dark page, stop and let the child sit in it. The silent beat
      does the teaching. If you rush to explain, you take the work out of the
      child's hands.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Point it out in the room.</strong> After the book, name one pair you
      can see right now. The warm lamp against the cool window, the loud water and
      the quiet floor. One real example does more than a whole page of commentary.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ask the one question.</strong> What does this need its opposite for?
      A four-year-old may shrug. A seven-year-old will look at the dark and answer
      that it is what makes the light show. Give it time.
    </li>
  </ul>

